{"meta": {"chapter_title": "Foundations of CBI and Design Tensions", "title": "CBI as Pedagogical Judgment", "description": "CAT 531 textbook section connecting cbi as pedagogical judgment to Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "course": "cat531-supplement", "chapter": "01", "section": "01", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."], "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "prereq_section_ids": [], "practice_ids": ["cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_artifact_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_annotation_signal", "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_ai_boundary", "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_calibration"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 1, "top_tier_hardened": false}, "content": "# CBI as Pedagogical Judgment\n\n![CBI as Pedagogical Judgment textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/01-01-cbi-as-pedagogical-judgment.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nComputer-based instruction (CBI) is often sold as a question of features: which platform grades faster, adapts more, or engages students longer. That framing hides the part that actually belongs to you. The pedagogical question is not \"is this tool good?\" but \"does putting a computer between me and this learning goal change what my students can do, and at what cost to whom?\" That question has no vendor-supplied answer. It is a judgment, and judgment is the thing this course holds you accountable for.\n\nTreating CBI as judgment matters because the alternative, treating it as procurement, quietly transfers authority over learning to whoever designed the software. When you cannot articulate why a tool serves a specific goal for specific students, you have not selected a tool; you have deferred a decision. This section establishes the habit that runs through the whole module: every technology choice you defend should be reconstructable by a colleague who was not in the room.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision shows a defensible decision in CAT 531.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA 9th-grade biology teacher has 28 students, a free AI quiz-generator that promises to cut three hours of grading a week, and a unit test in nine days. The tool stores student responses on a vendor server with no published retention period, and two families have already filed third-party-processor opt-outs. The exciting question (\"will instant feedback motivate them?\") is the wrong one to lead with. The professional question is whether she can name the instructional purpose, the equity-and-privacy boundary, and the evidence that would let a colleague call her decision sound rather than convenient.\n\nThis is the shape of nearly every CBI decision a teacher faces: a real benefit, a real risk, and a deadline that pressures you to skip the reasoning. The section exists to slow that moment down.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nPedagogical judgment about CBI is the disciplined act of deciding, with reasons a peer can audit, whether and how a computer should mediate a particular piece of learning. It is not intuition and it is not compliance with a recommendation. It is a structured weighing of four elements that must all be named explicitly:\n\n- **Purpose**: the specific thing a learner should be able to do that the tool is supposed to serve. \"Surface misconceptions before the summative\" is a purpose; \"save time\" and \"boost engagement\" are benefits, not purposes.\n- **Constraint**: the condition that limits or forbids the obvious choice. Class size, a privacy opt-out, an unstated retention clause, a device shortage.\n- **Evidence**: the specific source that makes the call defensible. A clause in a privacy policy, a district vetted-tools list, a spot-check of outputs.\n- **Revision**: what changed once the evidence was considered, including what you decided NOT to do.\n\nThree scholarly anchors keep this from collapsing into opinion, and they are deliberately in tension:\n\n| Anchor | What it makes you ask | Where it pushes the decision |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) | Does the technology actually fit THIS content and THIS pedagogy together, not just generically? | Toward purpose-fit |\n| Critical edtech studies | Whose burden, surveillance, or exclusion does this tool create that the sales pitch omits? | Toward naming hidden costs |\n| [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017) | What happens to student data, and does the model's training match my learners? | Toward boundaries and privacy |\n\nA choice that satisfies one anchor while ignoring the others is not yet a judgment. The friction between them is the work.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A teacher writes: 'I will adopt QuizGenAI because it saves three hours a week.' Which element of judgment is she treating a benefit as?\" explanation=\"Time saved is a real benefit, but it is not the instructional purpose. The purpose names what a learner should be able to do (surface misconceptions before the summative); a benefit cannot justify the tool unless the purpose is also met.\" options='[{\"text\":\"She is naming the Constraint\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"She is collapsing a benefit into the Purpose\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"She is citing Evidence\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"She is documenting a Revision\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nScenario: the 9th-grade biology teacher from the opening case decides whether to adopt the free AI quiz-generator. Walk the judgment in four numbered moves.\n\n**Before (preference framing).** The teacher's first draft of the adoption note reads:\n\n> I want to use QuizGenAI because it saves me about three hours a week and students said the instant feedback feels motivating.\n\n1. **Locate the decision.** This is not \"is the tool good?\" It is \"does adopting this tool serve a specific learning purpose without creating an unacceptable student-data risk?\"\n2. **Name the instructional purpose.** Faster formative feedback on unit checks so misconceptions surface before the summative test. (Engagement and the 3-hour saving are benefits, not the purpose.)\n3. **Name the binding constraint.** Two of 28 students are on data-privacy flags from families who opted out of third-party processors; the retention period is unstated.\n4. **Weigh evidence and an alternative.** Evidence: the privacy policy has no retention clause; the district list of vetted tools does not include this one. Alternative weighed and rejected: a vetted but slower district tool that keeps data in-house.\n\n**After (judgment framing).** The revised note reads:\n\n> I am declining QuizGenAI for whole-class use and routing formative quizzes through the district-vetted tool instead. Purpose: surface misconceptions before the summative. Constraint: two students carry data-opt-out flags and QuizGenAI has no stated retention period. I rejected the faster QuizGenAI option because the time saving does not justify processing flagged students' responses on an unvetted server. I can revisit if the vendor publishes a retention clause.\n\nThe improvement is not a longer note. It is an inspectable trace: a colleague can now see the purpose, the constraint, the evidence, and the alternative that was set aside, and can challenge the call on its merits.\n\nWork the same decision as a live dilemma. At each branch, choose the move that protects students first and keeps the reasoning inspectable.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The 9-day deadline: adopting QuizGenAI\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Nine days to the unit test. QuizGenAI would save three grading hours a week, but two of your 28 students carry third-party-processor opt-outs and the tool has no stated retention period. What do you lead with?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Pilot it now for the whole class because the time saving is urgent\",\"next\":\"nA\",\"feedback\":\"You led with the benefit and skipped the constraint. The two opt-out flags and the unstated retention clause are unresolved, so the call is convenient, not defensible.\"},{\"label\":\"Name the instructional purpose and the binding constraint before judging the feature\",\"next\":\"nB\",\"feedback\":\"Correct order: purpose (surface misconceptions early) and constraint (two opt-out flags, no retention clause) come before the affordance.\"}]},\"nA\":{\"prompt\":\"A colleague asks how you protected the two flagged students. You realize their responses would sit on an unvetted server. What now?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep going; most students benefit\",\"next\":\"nLoss\",\"feedback\":\"Averaging the benefit across the class hides a concentrated, non-consenting harm to two students.\"},{\"label\":\"Stop and route flagged students through a vetted in-house tool\",\"next\":\"nWin\",\"feedback\":\"You recovered the judgment by protecting students first.\"}]},\"nB\":{\"prompt\":\"The district vetted-tools list does not include QuizGenAI, and a vetted but slower district tool keeps data in-house. Which call is inspectable?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Adopt QuizGenAI for everyone; revisit privacy later\",\"next\":\"nLoss\",\"feedback\":\"Deferring the privacy check after adoption is exactly the move that surrenders authority to the vendor.\"},{\"label\":\"Decline QuizGenAI for whole-class use, route quizzes through the vetted tool, and name the rejected alternative\",\"next\":\"nWin\",\"feedback\":\"Purpose met, constraint honored, evidence cited, road-not-taken named. A peer can reconstruct this call.\"}]},\"nWin\":{\"prompt\":\"Your note now states purpose, constraint, evidence, and the rejected faster option with its reason. The decision is reconstructable without you in the room.\",\"feedback\":\"This is CBI as judgment: a visible trace, not just an outcome.\",\"choices\":[]},\"nLoss\":{\"prompt\":\"The decision documents an outcome but hides the trade. A reviewer cannot tell whether you reasoned it through or just complied with convenience.\",\"feedback\":\"Revisit: protect students first, then make the rejected alternative visible.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen a CBI choice forces a value against an affordance, order your reasoning so students are protected first. Name the classroom value conflict before you discuss what the tool can do, weigh the technology affordance second, and confirm the policy boundary third. A tool choice is justified only when it protects students while improving a specific learning activity.\n\nA defensible CBI decision should survive three quick tests:\n\n1. **Purpose check:** What, specifically, should students be able to do that they could not before, and is the computer load-bearing for that?\n2. **Evidence check:** Which exact source (a policy clause, a vetted-tools list, an output spot-check) makes the call inspectable rather than asserted?\n3. **Revision check:** What did you decide against, and why did you reject it?\n\nIf you cannot answer all three, you have a preference dressed as a decision.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"In your own words, explain why a decision documented after the fact is NOT the same as a decision reasoned through, even when both produce the same final choice.\" answer=\"In this course, judgment is the visible trace, not the eventual outcome. A decision reasoned through shows the purpose, constraint, evidence, and rejected alternative as it was made, so a colleague can audit and challenge the reasoning. A post-hoc justification can reverse-engineer a tidy rationale for a choice that was actually made on convenience or habit, hiding whether students were ever protected. Two identical outcomes can rest on very different reasoning, and only the inspectable trace lets a reviewer tell them apart.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe most common error is treating a vendor recommendation or a popular adoption as evidence. \"Other schools use it\" describes diffusion, not fit; it tells you nothing about your purpose or your students' privacy posture. A second error is collapsing benefit into purpose: time saved is real, but it cannot justify a tool unless the learning goal is also met. A third is believing that a decision documented after the fact is the same as a decision reasoned through; in this course, judgment is the visible trace, not the eventual outcome.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the concept to a fresh case: a teacher considering an AI discussion-summarizer that condenses each class's online forum into three bullet points the next morning. Walk the judgment as a decision, not a list of terms.\n\nFirst, state the purpose precisely. Is the computer load-bearing here? The goal is \"help me notice which threads stalled so I can re-open them.\" A summarizer can serve that; a faster human skim might too. Naming the purpose this tightly already tells you the tool is convenience, not necessity, which lowers how much risk you should accept for it.\n\nSecond, run the anchors against it. [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge): does a summary actually support discussion pedagogy, or does it let the teacher skip reading student voice? Critical edtech studies: a summarizer trained on majority-register prose may flatten or misread multilingual students' contributions, quietly making their threads \"the ones that did not matter.\" Datafication: forum posts are now leaving the building. Each anchor surfaces a cost the feature list hid.\n\nThird, decide and show the trade. A defensible call: use the summarizer as a triage aid the teacher still reads behind, never as a substitute for reading flagged or short threads, and disable it if posts cannot stay inside district storage. The judgment is visible because the rejected option (trusting the summary outright) is named alongside the reason.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read, keep a two-column note: the **decision** on the left, the **evidence that makes it inspectable** on the right. For CBI judgment specifically, watch for the moment a benefit is being smuggled in as a purpose, and write down what the real purpose would have to be for the tool to be load-bearing.\n\nA strong reader of CBI as judgment can answer four questions:\n\n1. What learning goal is the computer actually serving, and is it necessary or merely convenient?\n2. Which constraint (privacy, equity, access) would forbid the obvious choice?\n3. What source would persuade a skeptical colleague that the call protects students?\n4. What would change if the vendor published a retention clause, or if the tool became mandatory?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nThe artifact is strong when a mentor teacher can read your decision trace and reconstruct the call without you in the room: purpose stated as a learning goal, the binding constraint named, the evidence cited, and at least one rejected alternative with its reason. Visual polish is the last layer and counts for nothing if the rejected alternative is missing, because a decision with no visible road-not-taken is indistinguishable from a preference.\n\nA useful closing line for the artifact sounds like: \"I declined the faster option because the time saving did not justify processing flagged students' data on an unvetted server; I will revisit if the vendor publishes a retention clause.\" That sentence shows the trade, the threshold, and the conditions for changing the call.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the same biology decision into a district that has just signed an enterprise data-processing agreement covering all major vendors. The purpose (surface misconceptions early) is unchanged, but the binding constraint dissolves: the two opt-out flags are now covered by the agreement, and retention is contractually defined. The defensible call may flip from \"decline\" to \"adopt with the leaderboard off.\" If your reasoning only produces \"decline,\" you memorized an outcome; if it produces a different but still-justified call, you understand CBI as judgment.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/01-01-cbi-as-pedagogical-judgment-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"01.01\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 01.01. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision captures instructional purpose, equity or privacy boundary, support request, revision rationale, and confidence change.\n\n## Concept Map\n\nSee how the four judgment elements and the three scholarly anchors connect for this section.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions\", \"cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment", "stem": "A teacher writes in a tool-adoption memo: 'I chose the adaptive math app because students find it engaging and the vendor demo was impressive.' What is the core problem with this as a pedagogical-judgment artifact?", "options": ["It names a learning purpose but no equity risk.", "It justifies the choice by appeal (engagement, demo polish) rather than by an instructional purpose, a constraint, and inspectable evidence.", "It cites the vendor, which is never allowed in CAT 531.", "It is too short to be a memo."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Pedagogical judgment requires naming the instructional purpose, the constraint that matters, and the evidence that makes the call defensible. Engagement and demo polish are preferences, not justification.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_judgment_as_tool_preference", "difficulty": "easy", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_foundations_of_cbi_and_design_tensions", "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment", "stem": "Two teachers face the same situation: a free quiz platform that improves grading speed but stores student responses on servers with an unclear data-retention policy. Teacher A adopts it; Teacher B declines. Under CBI as pedagogical judgment, when can BOTH be defensible decisions?", "options": ["Never; one of them must be objectively wrong.", "Only if the principal approves both.", "When each teacher names the same instructional purpose but weighs the student-data constraint against the workflow benefit differently and can show the reasoning.", "When both teachers agree to use the tool the same way."], "correct_index": 2, "explanation": "Judgment is defensible when the reasoning is inspectable, not when everyone reaches the same answer. Two teachers can weigh a real constraint differently and both be defensible if each shows purpose, constraint, and evidence.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_one_right_answer_fallacy", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment", "evidence_based_revision"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning", "stem": "A teacher asks an AI assistant whether a reading app is appropriate for a class with three multilingual learners and an IEP student. The AI returns a confident 'Yes, it is fully appropriate.' What is the defensible next move?", "options": ["Adopt the app, since the AI verified appropriateness.", "Treat the AI claim as one unverified input and check it against the named learners' specific needs and the privacy policy before deciding.", "Reject the app because AI cannot be trusted on any classroom question.", "Ask the AI a second time to confirm."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "An AI cannot hold the professional accountability for an equity or access decision. Its output is a proposal to be judged against the actual learners and policy, not an authority that ends the judgment.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_ai_holds_the_judgment", "difficulty": "medium", "concept_ids": ["ai_supported_learning", "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment"]}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision", "stem": "A teacher's adoption rationale lists the instructional purpose and the equity risk but stops there. A reviewer says it is still not defensible. What single element is missing that would make the judgment inspectable?", "options": ["A longer description of how engaging the tool is.", "The named evidence source plus what was rejected or modified, so a colleague can reconstruct why this choice beat the alternative.", "A statement that the teacher has used technology before.", "The price of the tool."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "Naming purpose and risk is necessary but not sufficient. A defensible judgment also points to the evidence used and the alternative weighed and set aside, so the reasoning can be reconstructed and challenged.", "misconception_id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_naming_risk_equals_resolving_it", "difficulty": "hard", "concept_ids": ["evidence_based_revision", "cat531_supplement_cbi_as_pedagogical_judgment"]}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "6daee110f837bb282e5f49c08defc6343adbf5f841f11a64f51e5e8d72b07dd2", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_judgment_as_tool_preference", "pattern": "judgment_collapses_to_preference", "description": "Treating a technology-adoption decision as a matter of taste, engagement, or demo polish rather than a defensible professional judgment", "trigger": "learner justifies a tool by 'students like it' or 'the demo was impressive' with no instructional purpose, constraint, or evidence", "feedback": "Restate the choice as a judgment: name the instructional purpose, the constraint that matters here, and the evidence that makes this call defensible.", "rail_action": "ask"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_one_right_answer_fallacy", "pattern": "single_correct_decision_assumption", "description": "Assuming a tool decision has one objectively correct answer rather than a defensible reasoning trace that can vary across contexts", "trigger": "learner insists one teacher must be wrong when two defensible teachers reach different decisions", "feedback": "Defensibility lives in the reasoning, not in agreement. Compare the two teachers' purpose, constraint weighting, and evidence rather than picking a single winner.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_ai_holds_the_judgment", "pattern": "ai_as_decision_authority", "description": "Letting an AI assistant's confident output stand as the professional decision about appropriateness or access", "trigger": "learner adopts a tool because an AI said it was appropriate, with no check against actual learners or policy", "feedback": "The teacher holds the accountability. Treat the AI output as one unverified input and judge it against the named learners and the data policy.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat531_supplement_1_1_naming_risk_equals_resolving_it", "pattern": "naming_substitutes_for_reasoning", "description": "Believing that naming an equity or privacy risk is the same as resolving it in a defensible way", "trigger": "learner lists a risk but never shows the evidence used or the alternative weighed and rejected", "feedback": "Add the inspectable layer: which evidence informed the call, and which alternative did you weigh and set aside, and why.", "rail_action": "practice"}]}